CONDITIONAL TEMPLATE COVERAGE
Apply these boundaries before treating a string, heading or named slot as a loss.

1. Complete cookie-category definitions shared by both sides preserve the notice's
retained basis. Generic introductory framing about optimizing the site, improving
experience, cookies/device storage, browsing behavior or IDs, and general effects
of consenting or withdrawing is harmless when those full definitions are already
retained. Decorative opening questions, synonyms, repeated headings and rewordings
do not add a separate purpose. A stock cookie paragraph already retained elsewhere in the same complete
notice is covered repetition when copied beneath another generic category
heading. Count its meaning at that retained occurrence, rather than inventing
a second category-specific rule from the new heading. In this stock-copy branch,
a copied generic statement about necessary basic functions or inability to
disable cookies does not become a new mandatory-state assertion for the other
category solely through that heading. Apply the covered-repetition branch before
general bound-subject or mandatory/optional conflict tests. An explicit additional
proposition naming a category's actual processing, necessity or consent condition,
new concrete deployment facts, or independently changed terms still matters;
it is outside the stock-copy branch. Compare complete propositions across the
whole keeper, including the original retained occurrence. On this branch,
the covered repeated side has no own-side retained loss; keep its coverage loss
ID empty. A real extra contribution on the opposite side keeps its own loss.
Distinguish a copied paragraph from an independently assigned state. A short
category-specific setting or toggle value asserts that category's actual
mandatory/optional condition even when rendered as UI. Non-main role, brevity,
and the word "setting" do not make that assertion harmless. A missing value is
not an opposite value, but can be a one-sided useful addition. This does not
reclassify a generic paragraph already retained elsewhere merely because it is
copied beneath a new heading; that is still the covered-stock-copy branch.
The generic introductory-framing exception requires actual shared complete
category definitions covering the claimed purposes. Without that prerequisite,
compare every stated purpose and mechanism directly; a broad "better experience"
claim does not entail a specific recognition, tracking or use condition. A common
cookie topic is not equivalent coverage. Ordinary synonyms that preserve every
actual claim can still be semantically covered without invoking this exception.
Retain genuinely additional, concrete deployment details, data flows, storage or
transfer restrictions, specific uses, retention periods, consent mechanisms,
prices or obligations. A concrete analytics deployment explanation can add useful
content while still fully covering a shorter notice that repeats a stock paragraph.
One-way coverage then follows the actual extra contribution; the repetition does
not create a conflict. This boundary requires the shared complete definitions or
an already covered repeated paragraph, not merely a common cookie-related topic.

